¶
'''averruncator''' (av′e-rung-kā″tọr), ''n.'' [< ''averruncate'' + ''-or;'' also spelled ''aberuncator:'' see ''aberuncate.''] See ''aberuncator.''

'''aversant''' (a̤-vėr′sa̤nt), ''a.'' [< L. ''aversan(t-)s'', ppr. of ''aversari'', turn away, < ''ā'' for ''ab'', away, + ''versari'', turn. Cf. ''averse.''] In ''her.'', turned to show the back: said of a right hand. Also called ''dorsed.''
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|A Hand Aversant or Dorsed.}}}}

'''aversation''' (av-ėr-sā′shọn), ''n.'' [< L. ''aversatio(n-)'', < ''aversari'', pp. ''aversatus:'' see ''aversant.''] Aversion; a turning away from. [Obsolete or rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>I had an ''aversation'' to this voyage
When first my brother moved it.</poem>
{{right|''Chapman'', Revenge of Bussy d’Ambois, iii. 1.}}
Certainly for a king himself to charge his subjects with high treason, and so vehemently to prosecute them in his own cause as to do the office of a searcher, argued in him no great ''aversation'' from shedding blood.
{{right|''Milton'', Eikonoklastes, ix.}}
Some men have a natural ''aversation'' to some vices or virtues and a natural affection to others. {{right|''Jer. Taylor.''}}}}

'''averse''' (a̤-vėrs′), ''a.'' [< L. ''aversus'', pp. of ''avertere'', turn away: see ''avert.''] '''1.''' Turned away from anything; turned backward; averted.
{{smaller block|<poem>Earth . . . with her part ''averse''
From the sun’s beam.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., viii. 138.}}
<poem>The tracks ''averse'' a lying notice gave,
And led the searcher backward from the cave.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Æneid, viii.}}}}
Hence—'''2.''' Specifically: (''a'') In ''bot.'', turned away from the central axis: opposed to ''adverse'' (which see). (''b'') In ''ornith.'', set back or turned away from: applied to pygopodous or rump-footed birds, whose legs are set so far back

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|<poem>''Averse'' alike to flatter, or offend.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Essay on Criticism, l. 743.}}}}
{{smaller block|As Mr. Wilmot knew that I could make a very handsome settlement on my son, he was not ''averse'' to the match.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Vicar, ii.}}}}
'''4.''' Unfavorable; indisposed; adverse.
{{smaller block|<poem>Some much ''averse'' I found and wondrous harsh,
Contemptuous, proud, set on revenge and spite.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', S. A., l. 1461.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>And Pallas now ''averse'' refused her aid. {{right|''Dryden.''}}</poem>}}
{{smaller|[This word and its derivatives are now regularly followed by ''to'', and not by ''from'', although the latter is used by some modern writers. The word itself includes the idea of ''from''; but the literal meaning is ignored, the affection of the mind signified by the word being regarded as exerted ''toward'' the object of dislike. Similarly, the kindred terms ''contrary'', ''repugnant'', etc., are also followed by ''to''.]='''Syn. 3.''' ''Averse'', ''Reluctant'', disinclined, backward, slow, loath, opposed. ''Averse'' implies habitual dislike or unwillingness, though not of a very strong character, and is nearly synonymous with ''disinclined'': as, ''averse'' to study, to active pursuits. ''Reluctant'', literally, struggling back from, implies some degree of struggle either with others who are inciting us on, or between our own inclination and some strong motive, as sense of duty, whether it operates as an impelling or as a restraining influence. See ''antipathy''.}}
{{smaller block|''Averse'' to pure democracy, yet firm in his regard for existing popular liberties. {{right|''Bancroft'', Hist. U. S., I. 277.}}}}
{{smaller block|I would force from the ''reluctant'' lips of the Secretary of State his testimony to the real power of the masses.
{{right|''W. Phillips'', Speeches, p. 44.}}}}
{{smaller|'''4.''' ''Adverse'', ''Inimical'', etc. See ''hostile''.}}

'''averse†''' (ạ-vėrs′), ''v. t.'' and ''i.'' [< L. ''aversus'': see the adj.] To turn away; avert. ''B. Jonson.''
{{smaller block|<poem>Wise Pallas’ shield
(By which, my face ''aversed'', in open field
I slew the Gorgon).</poem>
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Masque of Queens.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
¶
'''aversely''' (a̤-vėrs′li), ''adv.'' '''1.''' In the reverse or opposite direction; backward.—'''2.''' With aversion or repugnance; unwillingly. [Rare in both senses.]

'''averseness''' (a̤-vėrs′nes), ''n.'' [< ''averse'' + ''-ness.''] The state of being averse; opposition of mind; dislike; unwillingness; backwardness.

'''aversion''' (a̤-vėr′shon), ''n.'' [< L. ''aversio(n-)'', < ''avertere'': see ''averse'', ''a.'', and ''avert.''] '''1†.''' A turning away; a change of application.
{{smaller block|A figurative speech called apostrophe, which is an ''aversion'' of speech from one thing . . . to another.
{{right|''Bp. Morton'', Episcopacy Asserted, p. 101.}}}}
'''2†.''' The act of averting or warding off.—'''3.''' An averted state of the mind or feelings; opposition or repugnance of mind; fixed or habitual dislike; antipathy: used absolutely or with ''to'', sometimes with ''from'', ''for'', or ''toward.''
{{smaller block|His ''aversion towards'' the house of York. {{right|''Bacon.''}}
Adhesion to vice, and ''aversion from'' goodness.
{{right|''Bp. Atterbury.''}}
A state ''for'' which they have so great an ''aversion.''
{{right|''Addison.''}}
An ''aversion to'' a standing army in time of peace had long been one of the strongest of English sentiments.
{{right|''Lecky'', Eng. in 18th Cent., iii.}}}}
'''4†.''' Opposition or contrariety of nature: applied to inanimate substances.
{{smaller block|Magnesia, notwithstanding this ''aversion'' to solution, forms a kind of paste with water. {{right|''Fourcroy'' (trans.).}}}}
'''5.''' A cause of dislike; an object of repugnance.
{{smaller block|Had I no preference for any one else, the choice you have made would be my ''aversion.''
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Rivals, i. 2.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller|'''4†.''' To oppose; view with aversion.}}
{{smaller block|The nature of mankind doth certainly ''avert'' both killing and being kill’d.
{{right|''Decay Christ. Piety'' (1667), vi. § 9, 251. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}
'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To turn away. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|''Averting'' from our neighbour’s good.
{{right|''Thomson'', Spring, l. 301.}}}}

'''avertebrated''' (a-vėr′tē-brā-ted), ''a.'' [< Gr. ἀ- priv. (''a-''<sup>18</sup>) + ''vertebrated''.] Evertebrated; invertebrate.
{{smaller block|The Linnæan classification of ''avertebrated'' animals.
{{right|''G. Johnston'' (ed. of Cuvier, 1849), p. 335.}}}}

'''averted''' (a̤-vėr′ted), ''p. a.'' '''1.''' Turned away or aside.
{{smaller block|<poem>When food was brought to them, her share
To his ''averted'' lips the child did bear.</poem>
{{right|''Shelley'', Revolt of Islam, v. 30.}}}}
'''2.''' Specifically, in ''anat. drawing'', having the head of the object turned to the top of the figure. ''Wilder.''

'''averter''' (a̤-vėr′tėr), ''n.'' One who or that which averts or turns away.
{{smaller block|''Averters'' and purgers must go together, as tending all to the same purpose, to divert this rebellious humour [melancholy] and turn it another way.
{{right|''Burton'', Anat. of Mel., p. 407.}}}}

'''avertible''' (a̤-vėr′ti-bl), ''a.'' [< ''avert'' + ''-ible''.] Capable of being averted; preventable: as, “''avertible'' evils,” ''Kinglake.''

'''avertiment†''', ''n.'' An erroneous form of ''advertisement.'' ''Milton.''

